    "overview": "Scent is the game's narrative voice, not a minimap in disguise. The design has one rule: every stage of tracking must change WHAT the dog wants, not just WHERE the dog goes. The trail begins as a stranger's kindness (Maya's amber ribbon), deepens into a mystery (the blue-gray under-scent on her coat), becomes an excavation of grief (Errol's washed-out world), converts loss into a compass (air-scenting the park off one tennis ball), and finally inverts (the pound's scent-silence, broken by her arriving scent). Rain is the antagonist inside the mechanic — it erases the past in real time, forcing urgency in Act 1, delivering the gut-punch in Act 2, and justifying air-scenting thereafter. Day/night governs legibility: fresh trails live in all hours, old trails only in still dawn/dusk air, and night belongs to the catcher. Each scent source has a fixed color identity (Maya amber, Errol blue-gray, the park green, the pound dead-gray) so the endgame images — two ribbons in one frame at the fence, the confluence at the bench, color returning radially from cinnamon in the kennel — land without a single line of dialogue.",
